# Install any AnyMCP server

> AnyMCP is a catalog of MCP servers that are each one C# file. You save the file, and you
> point your MCP client at `dotnet run <file>.cs -v q`. There is no project file, no build
> step of your own, and no container. The only prerequisite is the .NET 10 SDK.

This page holds the parts that are the same for every server. Each server also has its own page with
the exact commands for that server. Start there:

- Catalog index for agents: {{ site.url }}/llms.txt
- Catalog as JSON: {{ site.url }}/servers.json
- One server: {{ site.url }}/servers/<id>/index.md

**The default is a project install.** The server file goes in the project, and the configuration goes
in the `.mcp.json` of the project. Nothing is written outside the project directory, and no other
project on the machine changes. Install for the user only when the user asks for it.

## Prerequisite: the .NET 10 SDK

```
dotnet --version
```

The output must start with `10.`. A file-based app with `#:package` directives is a .NET 10
SDK feature, so every earlier SDK fails, and the error does not always say why.

If `dotnet` is missing, or the version is below 10, install the SDK first:

- Windows: `winget install Microsoft.DotNet.SDK.10`
- macOS: `brew install --cask dotnet-sdk`, or the installer from https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download
- Linux: follow https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/install/linux for your distribution

Open a new shell after the install, so that `dotnet` is on the PATH.

## Step 1. Put the file in the project

From the root directory of the project:

```
mkdir -p .mcp-servers
curl -fsSL -o .mcp-servers/<file>.cs {{ site.url }}/servers/<id>/<id>.cs
```

In PowerShell, `curl` is a different command. Use `Invoke-WebRequest` instead:

```
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force .mcp-servers | Out-Null
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "{{ site.url }}/servers/<id>/<id>.cs" -OutFile ".mcp-servers/<file>.cs"
```

Commit the file. It is the whole server, so a person who clones the project needs nothing else.

## Step 2. Build one time, before you register the server

```
dotnet build .mcp-servers/<file>.cs -v q
```

The first build restores the NuGet packages and compiles. On a cold package cache this
takes from 30 seconds to two minutes. Most MCP clients give a stdio server about 30 seconds
to answer `initialize`, so a server that was never built reports a connection failure that
is only a timeout. Build one time by hand, and the first client start is fast.

**The build adds no file to the project.** A file-based app is not a project, so there is no `bin`
directory and no `obj` directory next to the `.cs` file. The SDK keeps the compiled output in a cache
of the user, under `%TEMP%/dotnet/runfile/` on Windows and `$TMPDIR/dotnet/runfile/` elsewhere, in a
directory named for the file and a hash of its content. So `.gitignore` needs no new line, and a
`git status` after a build shows only the `.cs` file you added.

For Claude Code you can also raise the startup limit instead, in milliseconds:

```
MCP_TIMEOUT=120000 claude
```

In PowerShell: `$env:MCP_TIMEOUT = "120000"; claude`

## Step 3. Register the server in the project

### Claude Code

```
claude mcp add <id> --scope project -- dotnet run ./.mcp-servers/<file>.cs -v q
```

`--scope project` writes `.mcp.json` in the root directory of the project. Commit that file, and
every person who clones the project gets the same server.

The `--` separator is required. Everything after it is the command that Claude Code runs.

Pass a secret by name, before the `--`. Claude Code expands `${...}` from the environment, so the
committed file holds no value:

```
claude mcp add <id> --scope project --env SOME_KEY=${SOME_KEY} -- dotnet run ./.mcp-servers/<file>.cs -v q
```

### A client that reads a JSON configuration file

Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, and Windsurf use the `mcpServers` key. Put this in `.mcp.json`
in the root directory of the project:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "<id>": {
      "type": "stdio",
      "command": "dotnet",
      "args": ["run", "./.mcp-servers/<file>.cs", "-v", "q"]
    }
  }
}
```

Visual Studio and VS Code use `servers` in place of `mcpServers`. The object inside is the
same.

The path is relative to the project. It stays correct for every person who clones the project, and on
every operating system, so it needs no Windows backslash and no absolute path. It resolves against
the working directory of the client, which is the project directory.

## Step 4. Restart the client, and verify

```
claude mcp list
claude mcp get <id>
```

The server must show as connected. Then ask the model to call one of its tools, and confirm
that a result comes back. A client with no CLI shows the same list in its own settings.

Claude Code asks a person to approve a project server the first time it reads `.mcp.json`. The prompt
exists so that a repository you clone cannot start a process on your machine without your agreement.

## Install for every project instead

Do this only when the user asks for it.

```
claude mcp add <id> --scope user -- dotnet run <ABSOLUTE_PATH>/<file>.cs -v q
```

At this scope the path must be absolute, because the working directory is different in each project.
Keep the file in a directory that stays, for example `~/.mcp-servers/`, and do not move it: the
configuration holds the path. In JSON on Windows each backslash of an absolute path is doubled, for
example `C:\Users\you\.mcp-servers\my-server.cs`.

`--scope local` is a third choice. It keeps the server in this project, and private to you, so the
project gets no `.mcp.json` entry to commit.

## Why `-v q`

Stdio transport uses standard output for the JSON-RPC messages. Without `-v q`, `dotnet run`
can write build output to standard output, and one such line breaks the protocol stream. The
client then reports a parse error, or shows no tools.

Keep `-v q` in every configuration. It is two array elements, `"-v"` and `"q"`, and not one
element `"-v q"`.

## Tool names

The C# SDK converts each method name to snake case. A method `GeneratePassword` is
`generate_password` on the wire. Each server page gives both names.

## Secrets

A server that needs a key lists it on its page. Every AnyMCP server starts and lists its
tools with no key set; the key is needed only when the model calls a tool. So a failure at
startup is never a missing key.

`.mcp.json` belongs to the project and goes into version control, so it must hold the name of a
variable and never a value.

## Remove a server

```
claude mcp remove <id> --scope project
```

Then delete `.mcp-servers/<file>.cs`.

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause |
|---|---|
| Connection times out on the first start | The build was never run. Do step 2, then register again. |
| Connected, but no tool appears | The SDK is below 10, or the path in `args` is wrong. |
| A JSON parse error in the client log | `-v q` is missing from `args`, so build output reached standard output. |
| `dotnet: command not found` | The .NET 10 SDK is not installed, or not on the PATH. |
| The server works for you and not for a colleague | They did not run step 2. The build cache belongs to one user. |
| The server stopped working after a move | A relative path follows the project. An absolute path at user scope does not. |
